issue_context.body:
Over the past week or so I started to learn how V8 optimizes property access through shapes and inline caches by following the blog post link in the decorators documentation ([Shapes and IC](https://mathiasbynens.be/notes/shapes-ics) + [Optimizing Prototypes](https://mathiasbynens.be/notes/prototypes)). After reading this and other information I found about this topic, I was curious if the following scenario would deoptimize the example given in the decorator documentation.

In the decorator documentation, it states that the following should be avoided:

```js
fastify.addHook('preHandler', function (req, reply, done) {
  req.user = 'Bob Dylan'
  done()
})

// Use the attached user property in the request handler.
fastify.get('/', function (req, reply) {
  reply.send(`Hello, ${req.user}`)
})
```

Instead, a user should decorate the request prototype by using a decorator like so:

```js
fastify.decorateRequest('user', '')

fastify.addHook('preHandler', (req, reply, done) => {
  req.user = 'Bob Dylan'
  done()
})

fastify.get('/', (req, reply) => {
  reply.send(`Hello, ${req.user}!`)
})
```

After reading about how V8 optimizes things like property access and function calls, I realized that this example can be optimized since the same "shape" request is being passed to the route handler. Since the same shape is always passed in this example, then property lookup can take advantage of inline caches and the function can be optimized.

What I'm curious about is what would happen if the user string was conditionally set in the `preHandler` hook:

```js
fastify.decorateRequest('user', '')

fastify.addHook('preHandler', (req, reply, done) => {
  if (condition) {
    req.user = 'Bob Dylan'
  }
  done()
})

fastify.get('/', (req, reply) => {
  reply.send(`Hello, ${req.user}!`)
})
```

This would mean that based on the condition, the shape of the object going into the route handler would be different. I read [here](https://itnext.io/v8-function-optimization-2a9c0ececf5e) that after a function receives too many different shapes, it stops filling inline caches. I'm curious about a couple of things in this scenario:

1. Should conditional property setting be avoided in these types of scenarios? If that's the case, wouldn't always setting the user string in the prehandler and omitting the empty user string on the request prototype have the same performance boost from inline caches since the same shape would always reach the handler?
2. Does setting the empty user string on the request prototype instead of leaving it unset prevent repeated object property lookups by forcing there to be a "fallback" value sitting in an inline cache for each shape? When the request prototype property value is not set (ie. when decorateRequest is not used), does V8 repeatedly attempt to look up the property value in the prototype chain, or is a special null value placed in the inline cache to prevent this lookup?
3. Are there any other deoptimizations that occur by having this conditional value? Does it make it harder for TurboFan to optimize the route handler function?
4. Are there any conventions we should follow with handling decorated properties that will speed up our applications?

Thanks!

comments:
Just to clarify, by shape I meant the hidden class that the request object points to in the V8 engine when it enters the route handler, not the overall properties that the request object has when it enters the route handler.

I just tested it and the hidden class is not the same. I created the following test code:

```js
const fastify = require('fastify');

const app = fastify();

let request;

app.decorateRequest('user', '');

app.addHook('preHandler', (req, reply, done) => {
  if (req.headers['x-user']) {
    req.user = 'User';
  }
  done();
});

app.get('/', (req, reply) => {
  const isSame = request ? %HaveSameMap(request, req) : null;

  request = req;
  console.log(isSame);
  reply.send(String(isSame));
});

app.listen(3000, (err, address) => {
  console.log(`Running on ${address}`);
});
```

Essentially it's keeping a copy of the previous request so that it can check if the next request that comes into the route handler has the same hidden class. It allows the `user` property to be set on the request instance if the `X-User` header is present in the request.

I ran the server using the `--allow-natives-syntax` so that the `%HaveSameMap` function can work:

```
$ node --allow-natives-syntax test.js
```

The following requests were made to the server:

```js
// Initialize the request object.
fetch('/', { headers: { } });

// Test that the request object has the same hidden class as the previous one.
fetch('/', { headers: { } });

// Test if the hidden class changes on the dynamically set property.
fetch('/', { headers: { 'X-User': 'Value' } });

// Test if the hidden class remains the same.
fetch('/', { headers: { 'X-User': 'Value' } });
```

I received the following output to the terminal:

```
$ node --allow-natives-syntax test.js                                                                                                                                                                                              
Running on http://127.0.0.1:3000
null
true
false
true
```

To clarify what each value means:

- `null`: This was the request object being set to compare on the next request.
- `true`: This states that the request object uses the same hidden class as the initial request.
- `false`: This states that the hidden class changes when the user string is dynamically set.
- `true`: This states that the hidden class remains the same when the dynamically set property is used twice in a row.

Now, if I remove the `decorateRequest` call and set the user property concretely:

```js
const fastify = require('fastify');

const app = fastify();

let request;

app.addHook('preHandler', (req, reply, done) => {
  req.user = 'User';
  done();
});

app.get('/', (req, reply) => {
  const isSame = request ? %HaveSameMap(request, req) : null;

  request = req;
  console.log(isSame);
  reply.send(String(isSame));
});

app.listen(3000, (err, address) => {
  console.log(`Running on ${address}`);
});
```

Then perform the same thing again, I get this:

```
$ node --allow-natives-syntax test.js                                                                                                                                                                                             
Running on http://127.0.0.1:3000
null
true
true
true
```

Which leads me to believe that setting the user string on the request prototype is a way to limit expensive object property lookups for inline caching by having a "fallback" hidden class for inline caching. The only issue is that if there's too many hidden classes entering the route handler due to dynamic properties not being set on the request, then, to my understanding, V8 will deoptimize and not use inline caching because it received too many different hidden classes and doesn't want to keep a whole bunch of different inline caches laying around.

My questions have updated to the following:

- Am I correct about using the request prototype value as a "fallback" hidden class for inline caching or is there other optimizations that V8 does that I'm not aware of?
- Should conditional properties in hooks be avoided in favor of concrete values? If we set `null` in the decorated property, should we set null in the hook as well to prevent handlers down the line from being potentially deoptimized?
